# Herdr Mobile Relay configuration. Copy to your plugin config dir as `.env`:
#   install -m 600 .env.example "$(herdr plugin config-dir rad.herdr-mobile-relay)/.env"
# All values are optional; the defaults suit a single-user, tailnet-only setup.

# --- Networking ---
# Port the bridge listens on (loopback only; tailscale serve proxies to it).
HERDR_RELAY_PORT=8787
# Bind host. Non-loopback values are rejected because proxy identity headers are only trustworthy
# behind a loopback listener.
HERDR_RELAY_HOST=127.0.0.1
# How the bridge is published on the tailnet (read by relay-ctl.sh when it runs `tailscale serve`,
# not by the bridge itself): "https" (default, Tailscale-managed cert) or "http"
# (plain HTTP for Headscale / `.internal` domains without HTTPS certs; then set
# HERDR_RELAY_PUBLIC_HOSTS below).
# HERDR_RELAY_SERVE_MODE=https
# Dedicated tailnet listener. Defaults to HERDR_RELAY_PORT so an existing HTTPS :443 Serve mapping is
# preserved. Set 443 only when Herdr Mobile Relay should own the root MagicDNS URL.
# HERDR_RELAY_SERVE_PORT=8787

# --- Herdr connection ---
# Usually injected by the systemd unit; override only if your socket is elsewhere.
# HERDR_SOCKET_PATH=/home/you/.config/herdr/herdr.sock
# Poll cadence (ms) and scrollback lines pulled for the detail view.
HERDR_RELAY_POLL_MS=1500
HERDR_RELAY_READ_LINES=200
# Keys sent to submit a reply after the text (comma-separated). Agent-dependent.
HERDR_RELAY_SUBMIT_KEYS=Enter
# Multi-session support (default on). One bridge fronts every running herdr session it finds under
# the config root (<HERDR_SOCKET_PATH dir>/herdr.sock + sessions/<name>/herdr.sock). Set "off" (or
# 0/false) to pin the bridge to the primary session only — no discovery, identical to the
# pre-multi-session behaviour.
# HERDR_RELAY_MULTI_SESSION=on

# --- Host folder picker (optional, Linux only in this increment) ---
# Explicit roots attest these directories share the local filesystem/user context of Herdr.
# Default [] exposes nothing. No symlink components, including in configured roots.
# macOS folder operations fail explicitly until safe descriptor-relative support is implemented.
# HERDR_RELAY_BROWSE_ROOTS=[{"name":"Workspaces","path":"/home/you/workspaces"}]

# --- Security ---
# Remote requests always require a non-empty identity header injected by Tailscale Serve (or an
# equally trusted private proxy). Choose ONE policy below; leaving all of them unset fails closed
# for remote access while loopback tools remain available.
#
# Single user (backward-compatible):
# HERDR_RELAY_TRUSTED_USER=you@example.com
#
# Multiple exact Tailscale login names (comma-separated). This combines with HERDR_RELAY_TRUSTED_USER
# when both are set:
# HERDR_RELAY_TRUSTED_USERS=you@example.com,teammate@example.com
#
# Any authenticated tailnet user. This is NOT anonymous or Internet access: requests still need
# Tailscale Serve's injected identity, must pass tailnet ACLs and Host/origin checks, and must never
# be published through Tailscale Funnel. Treat it as granting remote-shell-equivalent relay access
# to every tailnet identity allowed by your Tailscale policy:
# HERDR_RELAY_ALLOW_ANY_TAILNET_USER=on
#
# Generic authenticated proxies can override the identity header they strip and inject.
# HERDR_RELAY_TRUSTED_USER_HEADER=Tailscale-User-Login
#
# Optional per-device authorisation (OFF by default). When Herdr Mobile Relay sits behind a reverse proxy that
# authenticates the device and injects its identity as a request header, name that header here to
# trust and enforce it. Proxy-agnostic — the value is just an opaque device id; the bridge doesn't care
# how the proxy derived it. The header is trusted only because the bridge binds loopback behind the
# proxy (a direct client can't set it). Leave unset to disable.
# HERDR_RELAY_DEVICE_HEADER=X-Device-Id
# Device ids allowed to perform sensitive actions (typing into agents, creating tabs/spaces). Any
# other device carrying the header is read-only. A missing header is authorised only on loopback;
# on a public Host it is read-only so a proxy omission fails closed.
# To revoke a device, drop its id from this list and `systemctl --user restart rad-herdr-mobile-relay`. With the
# header set but this list empty, EVERY device is read-only (fail-closed).
# HERDR_RELAY_DEVICE_ALLOWLIST=my-phone,my-laptop
# Extra allowed browser origins (comma-separated, each a FULL origin incl. scheme).
# The bridge is same-origin only: a request passes when the browser's Origin host equals the Host
# header the bridge receives. Loopback exceptions apply only while HERDR_RELAY_HOST itself is loopback.
# A plain `tailscale serve` on your MagicDNS
# name matches automatically, so you DON'T need this for a normal Tailscale setup.
# You DO need it when a *different* public hostname or an extra reverse proxy / TLS terminator fronts
# Herdr Mobile Relay (a custom/vanity domain, a load balancer, Headscale + upstream TLS) — the public origin then
# no longer matches the forwarded Host and API calls return 403 "cross-origin rejected". Add that
# exact origin here. e.g.:
# HERDR_RELAY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://relay.example.com
#
# Host allowlist (comma-separated host[:port] values). Every non-loopback request must match this
# list or a HERDR_RELAY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS host. The launcher derives the normal Tailscale MagicDNS host
# automatically; direct/custom deployments must set it explicitly. Empty means loopback-only.
# Reverse proxies must add `Forwarded`, `X-Forwarded-For`, or `X-Forwarded-Host`; Tailscale Serve's
# injected identity header also supplies provenance. Forwarded requests never receive loopback
# operator exemptions, even if a client forges `Host: 127.0.0.1`.
# A proxy that rewrites the upstream Host must set `X-Forwarded-Host` to the original public host.
# HERDR_RELAY_PUBLIC_HOSTS=relay.your-tailnet.ts.net
